{"name":"Yellow Bonnet","hex":"#f9f6e8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"},{"term":"bonnet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of hat, once widely worn by women or children, held in place by ribbons tied under the chin; now uncommonly worn except in certain religious sects.","A traditional Scottish woollen brimless cap; a bunnet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put a bonnet on.","To take off the bonnet or cap as a mark of respect; to uncover."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bonnet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bonnet (headgear)","blocks":[{"text":"Bonnet has been used as the name for a wide variety of headgear for women since the Middle Ages. As with \"hat\" and \"cap\", it is impossible to generalize styles for which the word has been used, there is a tendency to describe it having soft material and lacking a brim, or at least one all the way round, rather than just at the front. Yet the term has also been used, for example, for steel helmets in Scotland (1505), where it has long been especially popular."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Old_woman_in_sunbonnet_by_Doris_Ulmann.jpg/330px-Old_woman_in_sunbonnet_by_Doris_Ulmann.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonnet_(headgear)"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg/500px-M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg/500px-M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bonnet decorated with lace and tulle from the 1880s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/JaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg/250px-JaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/JaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/JaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg/250px-JaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1804 watercolour of Jane Austen wearing a bonnet, by her sister, Cassandra.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJaneAustenCassandraWatercolour.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg/500px-Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg/1280px-Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg/500px-Woman%27s_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Woman's Calash, c.1825. Green silk. Los Angeles County Museum of Art  collection, M.87.93","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWoman's_Calash_LACMA_M.87.93.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Magasin_f%C3%B6r_konst%2C_nyheter_och_moder_1838%2C_illustration_nr_24.jpg/500px-Magasin_f%C3%B6r_konst%2C_nyheter_och_moder_1838%2C_illustration_nr_24.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Magasin_f%C3%B6r_konst%2C_nyheter_och_moder_1838%2C_illustration_nr_24.jpg/1280px-Magasin_f%C3%B6r_konst%2C_nyheter_och_moder_1838%2C_illustration_nr_24.jpg?utm_so